 spreads widened 30bp in Q3 to 165bp, so funding is pricier but still available.\n- **Equity is the weak point.** Two utility share offerings were cut back. Pellham Power withdrew its C$2.4B equity offering after demand fell short (pellham-offering).\n- **Pellham is the stress case.** It plans an 18% capex increase for 2027 and is the largest single buyer of grid equipment. It has now said it will cut 2027 transformer orders by about 40% and defer two substation programmes until financing improves. Debt alone is evidently not enough for Pellham, at least without more leverage.\n\n**Conclusion:** Investment-grade issuers like Norvane can use debt for their plans. Pellham apparently can't plug the gap with debt, and it is already cutting spending. Expect 2027 capex to be partly funded by debt, with some deferrals at the more stretched utilities.\n\n**Gaps:** The sources give no data on utility leverage, credit ratings, Pellham's debt capacity, or total 2027 funding needs. I'm inferring Pellham's constraint from its decision to cut orders rather than borrow more. Treat the conclusion as moderate-confidence.\n\nSources: utility-capex-2027, utility-credit, pellham-offering.","ttft_ms":3352,"type":"result","duration_ms":17086,"uuid":"bc87ce2f-75fb-4dd8-868f-ddd90377ffee","ttft_stream_ms":2855,"time_to_request_ms":144,"time_to_request_phases_ms":{"other":21,"system_prompt":5,"resume_other":0,"process_user_input":3,"input_attachments":34,"input_hooks":5,"conversation_append":1,"transcript_persist":3,"tool_pool_refresh":0,"init_message":13,"init_frame_send":2,"engine_pickup":1,"loop_entry":5,"message_prep":3,"model_config_wait":5,"autocompact":2,"request_announcements":9,"query_setup":1,"model_gates":6,"tool_schema_build":4,"message_normalization":4,"client_creation":10,"request_build":7},"turn_start_resume_kind":"none","process_turn_index":0,"time_to_request_loop_lag_ms":85,"time_to_request_cpu_ms":65,"time_to_request_major_faults":0,"process_rss_mib_turn_start":197,"proces